  ],
  "text": "Europa may hold more liquid water than every ocean on Earth put together, under kilometres of ice. It orbits Jupiter, far outside anywhere sunlight could keep water liquid. So the obvious question is what is heating it. Jupiter is. The same way our Moon moves the tides, only enormously stronger. Europa's orbit is not a perfect circle, so the squeeze changes as it goes round. That flexing makes heat inside it, continuously, with no sunlight involved at all. Enceladus does the same at Saturn, and it vents plumes straight out into space. We flew a spacecraft through one and found water, salts and organic molecules. Which means you do not have to drill. You can catch the ocean on the way past. The best place to look for life might not need a star at all. Only something to squeeze it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
